        "A layered self-portrait of the referenced woman, with the supplied image guiding her visible face and hair. Preserve her short dark bob, separated wispy fringe and natural facial proportions. She is in a narrow carousel restoration bay at blue hour, and she takes her own photograph while showing the small failure that has just been repaired. A white wooden carousel horse stands on a padded workbench behind her right shoulder. Its red carved mane panel has been returned to its place along the neck; a small brass clamp bridges the newly joined seam. At the lower-right foreground, the subject attached hand extends toward the camera position: her spare left hand holds a hand-length broken wooden joining pin toward the camera, its red paint stripe and pale splintered end connecting visually to that repaired red mane. The pin is large enough to recognize as painted broken wood. Fine pale wood dust on the bench, a used sanding block and a warm test bulb beside the horse make the ongoing restoration tangible. Her face sits near the center of the frame, and her eyes meet the lens with a tired, pleased half-smile, one corner of the mouth rising as if quietly sharing the repair's ridiculous little victory. A faint dusty flush, a loose strand beside her cheek and the soft fall of an indigo linen work shirt give her an approachable everyday presence. The open shirt collar and rolled sleeves belong to the working moment. Her left elbow remains bent close to her torso; her thumb and index finger pinch the intact end of the broken pin, while her other fingers curl naturally behind it. At this pinch, the offered object follows the visible subject hand contact; the red-striped wood projects forward from her fingers and its splintered end remains distinct. The nearest left hand occupies the lower-right foreground, clear of her face and of the visible clamp behind her; the near object leaves the requested facial expression region readable, keeping her eyes and tired half-smile